<h2>What "flattening" means</h2>
<p>
  A PDF form field — a text box, checkbox, dropdown — is a distinct object layered on top of the page, separate from the page's underlying content. That's what makes it editable: a PDF viewer renders the field, but the value you typed lives in the field object, not baked into the page itself. Flattening takes whatever value is currently in each field and permanently merges it into the page's fixed content — the same way any other text or graphic on the page is rendered. After flattening, there's no field object anymore, just content that looks the same but can't be edited through the form interface, because the form interface no longer exists.
</p>
<p>
  The same idea applies to annotations — comments, highlights, sticky notes, drawn markup added in a PDF viewer. These also exist as separate, editable layers by default; flattening merges them into the page too.
</p>

<h2>Why this matters</h2>
<p>
  An unflattened, filled-in form is still technically a blank form with values sitting in its fields. Anyone who opens it in a PDF editor can change those values without any visible indication that they've been altered — which is fine for an internal draft, but a real problem for anything meant to be a fixed record of what was submitted: a signed application, a declaration, a form with legal or contractual weight. Flattening converts the filled values into the same kind of fixed content as any other text on the page, so what was submitted is what stays submitted.
</p>
<p>
  It's also useful more narrowly: some systems and printers handle flattened PDFs more predictably than ones with live interactive elements, since there's no interactive layer for the rendering software to reconcile.
</p>

<h2>The tradeoff: it's not reversible</h2>
<p>
  Once flattened, the form fields are gone — you can't go back and edit a single answer without starting over from an unflattened copy. If there's any chance you or someone else will need to revise an answer later, keep the original unflattened file alongside the flattened one you actually submit or distribute.
</p>

<h2>Privacy: what happens to your file</h2>
<p>
  Flattening runs entirely in your browser using pdf-lib — your document is never uploaded.
</p>

